    q: "What is the bias-variance tradeoff?",
    level: 'Basics',
    a: "Bias is error from a model that is too simple and misses the pattern (underfitting). Variance is error from a model that is too sensitive to the training data and changes a lot with new samples (overfitting). Making a model more complex lowers bias but raises variance, so I look for the sweet spot, usually by checking validation scores across model complexity.",
    code: "import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score, KFold\n\nrng = np.random.RandomState(0)\nX = np.sort(rng.rand(40, 1) * 6, axis=0)\ny = np.sin(X).ravel() + rng.randn(40) * 0.2\n\nfor degree in [1, 4, 15]:\n    model = make_pipeline(PolynomialFeatures(degree), LinearRegression())\n    cv = KFold(n_splits=5, shuffle=True, random_state=0)\n    score = cross_val_score(model, X, y, cv=cv,\n                            scoring=\"neg_mean_squared_error\").mean()\n    print(f\"degree={degree:2d}  CV MSE={-score:.3f}\")",
