    level: 'Basics',
    a: "StandardScaler changes each feature to have mean 0 and standard deviation 1, which suits models like logistic regression, SVM and PCA. MinMaxScaler squeezes values into a fixed range, usually 0 to 1, which is handy for neural networks or image pixels, but it is sensitive to outliers. Scaling matters for distance and gradient based models; tree models don't need it.",
    code: "import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler, MinMaxScaler\n\nX = np.array([[10.0], [20.0], [30.0], [100.0]])\n\nstd = StandardScaler().fit_transform(X)\nmm = MinMaxScaler().fit_transform(X)\n\nprint(\"Standard:\", np.round(std.ravel(), 3))\nprint(\"  mean =\", round(std.mean(), 3), \" std =\", round(std.std(), 3))\nprint(\"MinMax:  \", np.round(mm.ravel(), 3))",
