    a: "If 95% of transactions are normal, a model that always says 'normal' gets 95% accuracy but catches zero fraud. So I look at precision, recall, F1 or PR-AUC instead. To handle imbalance I use class_weight='balanced', resample (oversample the minority or undersample the majority, e.g. SMOTE), tune the decision threshold, and always stratify my splits.",
    code: "from sklearn.datasets import make_classification\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.dummy import DummyClassifier\nfrom sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score, recall_score\n\nX, y = make_classification(n_samples=2000, weights=[0.95],\n                           class_sep=0.5, random_state=0)\nX_tr, X_te, y_tr, y_te = train_test_split(X, y, stratify=y, random_state=0)\n\nmodels = {\n    \"always majority\": DummyClassifier(),\n    \"plain logistic\": LogisticRegression(),\n    \"balanced logistic\": LogisticRegression(class_weight=\"balanced\"),\n}\nfor name, m in models.items():\n    p = m.fit(X_tr, y_tr).predict(X_te)\n    print(f\"{name:17s} accuracy={accuracy_score(y_te, p):.3f} \"\n          f\"recall={recall_score(y_te, p):.3f}\")",
