    q: "What are MAE, MSE, RMSE and R² for regression?",
    level: 'Intermediate',
    a: "MAE is the average absolute error and is easy to explain in the target's units. MSE averages squared errors, so it punishes big mistakes more; RMSE is its square root, bringing it back to the original units. R² tells me what share of the variance the model explains, where 1 is perfect and 0 is no better than predicting the mean.",
    code: "import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error, mean_squared_error, r2_score\n\ny_true = np.array([100, 150, 200, 250, 300])\ny_pred = np.array([110, 140, 210, 240, 330])\n\nmse = mean_squared_error(y_true, y_pred)\nprint(\"MAE :\", mean_absolute_error(y_true, y_pred))\nprint(\"MSE :\", mse)\nprint(\"RMSE:\", round(np.sqrt(mse), 3))\nprint(\"R2  :\", round(r2_score(y_true, y_pred), 3))",
