    q: "What is regularization? L1 vs L2?",
    level: 'Intermediate',
    a: "Regularization adds a penalty for large weights to the loss, which keeps the model simpler and reduces overfitting. L1 (Lasso) penalizes absolute values and can push some weights to exactly zero, so it also does feature selection. L2 (Ridge) penalizes squared values and shrinks all weights smoothly. The strength is controlled by alpha (or C, its inverse), which I tune with cross-validation.",
    code: "import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression, Lasso, Ridge\n\nrng = np.random.RandomState(0)\nX = rng.randn(50, 5)\ny = 3 * X[:, 0] + 2 * X[:, 1] + rng.randn(50) * 0.5   # only 2 useful features\n\nfor name, m in [(\"Plain\", LinearRegression()),\n                (\"L2 Ridge\", Ridge(alpha=10)),\n                (\"L1 Lasso\", Lasso(alpha=0.5))]:\n    m.fit(X, y)\n    print(f\"{name:9s}\", np.round(m.coef_, 2))",
    output: "Plain     [ 3.06  2.02  0.01 -0.03 -0.04]\nL2 Ridge  [ 2.57  1.63  0.04  0.07 -0.08]\nL1 Lasso  [ 2.61  1.43  0.    0.   -0.  ]",
