    q: "What is gradient descent and the learning rate?",
    level: 'Intermediate',
    a: "Gradient descent is how most models learn: compute the gradient (slope) of the loss and take a small step in the opposite direction, again and again. The learning rate is the step size. Too small and training is very slow, too large and the loss jumps around or blows up, so I tune it and often use schedulers or optimizers like Adam.",
    code: "import numpy as np\n\nX = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4], dtype=float)\ny = 2 * X                       # true weight is 2\n\ndef train(lr, steps=20):\n    w = 0.0\n    for _ in range(steps):\n        grad = -2 * np.mean((y - w * X) * X)   # d(MSE)/dw\n        w -= lr * grad\n    return w\n\nfor lr in [0.001, 0.05, 0.2]:\n    print(f\"lr={lr:<6} final w={train(lr):.3f}\")",
    output: "lr=0.001  final w=0.522\nlr=0.05   final w=2.000\nlr=0.2    final w=-2097150.000",
