    q: "What are transformers and the attention mechanism?",
    level: 'Advanced',
    a: "A transformer is the architecture behind modern LLMs. Its key idea is self-attention: each token builds a query, key and value, scores how relevant every other token is (query dot key, scaled and softmaxed), and takes a weighted mix of their values. This lets the model look at the whole sequence at once and run in parallel, unlike RNNs.",
    code: "import numpy as np\n\nnp.random.seed(0)\ntokens = [\"the\", \"cat\", \"sat\"]\nd = 4\nX = np.random.randn(3, d)                       # token embeddings\nWq, Wk, Wv = (np.random.randn(d, d) for _ in range(3))\n\nQ, K, V = X @ Wq, X @ Wk, X @ Wv\nscores = Q @ K.T / np.sqrt(d)                   # scaled dot-product\nweights = np.exp(scores) / np.exp(scores).sum(axis=1, keepdims=True)\noutput = weights @ V\n\nprint(\"Attention weights (rows sum to 1):\")\nfor tok, row in zip(tokens, weights):\n    print(f\"{tok:4s}\", row.round(3))\nprint(\"Output shape:\", output.shape)",
    output: "Attention weights (rows sum to 1):\nthe  [0.001 0.999 0.   ]\ncat  [0.056 0.043 0.901]\nsat  [0.071 0.914 0.015]\nOutput shape: (3, 4)",
